Id: aud001w
Severity: WARN
Category: logging
Check: check_audit
References: auditd(8), audit.rules(7)
Controls: CIS 8.2, 8.5; NIST AU-2, AU-12; ISO 8.15

auditd, the Linux audit daemon, is not running. Without it the kernel's
audit events (logins, changes to accounts and to watched files, use of
privileges, programs run by root) are not recorded, or only the few
that reach the journal, mixed with everything else and without the
rules that say what to watch. Investigating a break-in then has little
to go on, and
compliance frameworks (the CIS benchmarks, PCI DSS requirement 10) ask
for an audit trail of this kind.

To fix it, install it (the auditd package on Debian and Ubuntu, audit
on Fedora, RHEL and SUSE), start it with 'systemctl enable --now
auditd', and load rules (see aud002w). On a desktop where nobody will
read the trail, accepting the finding with tigris-accept is reasonable.
